        "listName":  "Tai Long Village (Former Site Where the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade Carried Out Activities)",
        "paras":  [
                      "Tai Long Village is in the eastern part of Sai Kung, facing Tai Long Wan. Many villagers from Tai Long Village joined the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade, an anti-Japanese guerrilla force. The Chapel of the Immaculate Conception in the village was built in 1867 and was used as a Japanese military post during the Japanese Occupation. Japanese troops stayed in the chapel and forced villagers to dig air-raid shelters in the hill next to it. After the Japanese troops left, the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade stayed there. When the guerrillas moved on, they left the \"Little Devils\" team of young guerrillas in the village to stand guard against Japanese attacks. Life was very hard for the villagers at that time and food was short; two people even starved to death. Even so, the villagers gave the little rice they had to support the guerrillas and the War of Resistance.",
                      "In the spring of 1943, the Japanese army launched a large-scale \"mopping-up\" operation against the guerrilla areas. The leadership of the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade hid in a sea cave near the shore at Lo Wan, Tai Long Village. Enemy warships patrolled the sea and tightly blocked the main routes, and the guerrillas in the cave were short of food and water. The women of Tai Long Village carried loads on shoulder poles through the dark night, feeling their way along the rugged, winding mountain paths to deliver food to the guerrillas."
                  ]
