        "subtitle":  "Place Where Lu Feng, Deputy Commander of the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade, Recovered from Illness",
        "listName":  "Po Lin Monastery, Lantau Island (Place Where Lu Feng, Deputy Commander of the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade, Recovered from Illness)",
        "paras":  [
                      "Po Lin Monastery stands on the Ngong Ping Plateau of Lantau Island, between Lantau Peak and Nei Lak Shan. On the hill behind the monastery is a stone house where Lu Feng, Deputy Commander of the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade, recovered from illness during the War of Resistance. Lu Feng had first been recovering at a nunnery at Tei Tong Tsai, on the northern side of Lantau Peak. Later, news of his whereabouts leaked out and Japanese troops surrounded the nunnery to search it, so he moved to Po Lin Monastery to recover. In 1944, during a Japanese \"mopping-up\" operation on Lantau Island, Japanese troops came to search the monastery. Master Fa Ke, the abbot of Po Lin Monastery, disguised Lu Feng as a monk, and Lu Feng sat among several hundred monks and lay Buddhists listening to the abbot\u0027s scripture lecture in the \"Great Perfect Enlightenment\" Hall. After the lecture, the Japanese soldiers held a sword to Master Fa Ke\u0027s neck, beat him badly and demanded to know where Lu Feng was. Master Fa Ke stayed calm and said nothing, and in the end Lu Feng escaped danger."
                  ]
