        "subtitle":  "Former Site of the Eighth Route Army Hong Kong Office",
        "listName":  "18 Queen\u0027s Road Central (Former Site of the Eighth Route Army Hong Kong Office)",
        "paras":  [
                      "In January 1938, the Yuet Hwa Company opened on the second floor of 18 Queen\u0027s Road Central (now Pacific House, 20–20B Queen\u0027s Road Central). The company was in fact the Eighth Route Army Hong Kong Office. “Yuet Hwa Company” was only the office\u0027s public name, and its tea wholesale and retail business was a cover for the office\u0027s activities. The Eighth Route Army Hong Kong Office held high the banner of the Anti-Japanese National United Front. It organised and mobilised large amounts of supplies, professional and technical personnel and young overseas Chinese, and sent them back to the country to serve. The office was set up by the Yangtze Bureau of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China and Zhou Enlai, to make full use of Hong Kong\u0027s special strategic position and political environment and to win the sympathy and support of compatriots in Hong Kong and Macao, overseas Chinese and international friends for China\u0027s War of Resistance. Liao Chengzhi was the director of the office, and Lian Guan was in charge of its day-to-day work."
                  ]
