                      "Built between the 1910s and the 1920s, the Tin Hau Temple in Sai Kung was used as a liaison station of the Hong Kong-Kowloon Brigade during the Japanese Occupation. The guerrillas also opened a teahouse called “Sleepless Night” next to the temple (in present-day Sai Kung Main Street), which served as a liaison station and an intelligence station. On 27 April 1947, General Neil Ritchie, representing the British Army, presented a pennant bearing the Chinese words for “Loyalty, Courage, Honesty and Love” to the Sai Kung villagers in front of the Tin Hau Temple, in recognition of their anti-Japanese work.",
