        "listName":  "21 Seymour Road, Mid-Levels (Historic Site of the Headquarters of the China Defence League)",
        "paras":  [
                      "After the fall of Shanghai, Soong Ching-ling arrived in Hong Kong in late December 1937. Making use of Hong Kong\u0027s special status, she began to launch an anti-Japanese national salvation movement. On 14 June 1938, in the small sitting room of her home on Conduit Road, Hong Kong Island (now Scenic Villas), the China Defence League was formally founded. Its headquarters was at 21 Seymour Road, Mid-Levels, and its aims were “promoting resistance against Japan, winning international aid and supporting the War of Resistance”. Soong Ching-ling served as Chairperson of the League\u0027s Central Committee.",
                      "With the help of Liao Chengzhi and Pan Hannian of the Eighth Route Army Hong Kong Office, the League made great contributions to the War of Resistance. It promoted the setting up of China aid organisations in Europe, America, Southeast Asia and other places to provide aid to China. It also set up a children\u0027s relief fund and organised charity performances and charity sales to help Chinese orphans who had suffered in the war. In addition, the League told the world about China\u0027s War of Resistance and won the sympathy and support of other countries for China, building an internationalist bridge of friendship between the people of China and the peoples of the world."
                  ]
